data language_module_home_law: String = "WHERE A TARGET'S SURFACES LIVE IS ORGANIZATIONAL POLICY, NEVER IDENTITY (operator verdict relayed 2026-08-05, and the framing is load-bearing). DeclarationRef remains the single identity authority for a language target; a canonical_prefix says where that target's modules SHOULD be stored and decides nothing about what the target IS. The two must not fuse, because a path prefix is exactly the kind of positional fact that would start competing with the symbolic key if it ever became a discriminant — the same class DESIGN section 3 rules on when it says paths are discriminators, not gospel, and that a citation names a symbol rather than a position. WHAT KEEPS THEM SEPARATE IS THE DIRECTION THE PREFIX IS READ, not the absence of a prefix check. A prefix is matched against a home the CALLER SUPPLIES, to answer 'is this file where its declared target says it should be'. Nothing here maps a path to a target: there is deliberately no function that takes a path and returns whose it is, because that function is what would make position compete with the symbolic key. A module misfiled under another target's directory is therefore REFUSED, never silently reclassified as belonging to the directory it happens to sit in. AN EARLIER REVISION OF THIS SENTENCE SAID THE WALL PERFORMS NO PREFIX MATCH AT ALL, and that was true of the mechanism but only because the mechanism was incomplete: it decided by exact equality over the flat roster alone, which left a target-specific module filed under the WRONG target's directory unreachable — not flat, so never consulted. The premise behind that narrowing was also false, namely that std carries no prefix predicate; starts_with is a registered PrimitiveContract in std.primitives with a template row in std.algebra and live production use, verified two-sided by execution. The narrowing was a rationalised gap, which is worse than a wrong fact because a wrong fact gets caught. THE DEFECT THIS ADDRESSES: dag/extdeps/languages was meant to mean one directory per target holding that target's subject and its surfaces. D0 created twenty-five such directories, and four flat modules still sit directly beside them, so the directory currently means 'a subject-only island beside flat siblings' instead. react_jsx is the one that matters on principle rather than tidiness: that module implements JSX serialization while naming React, and D0's registry just separated those two subjects — JSX is registered as its own target with its own citation and React is not a target at all — so the fused flat path preserves a naming defect this repository's own registry has already exposed."

data language_module_home_scope_note: String = "WHAT THIS MODULE DOES AND DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO. IT DOES: declare a canonical home for EVERY registered target (twenty-five languages plus the ngspice simulator), declare the two directories under languages/ whose target this repository has never registered so legitimate work there is admitted rather than refused, carry the four legacy flat modules as an EXACT finite frontier with a dissolution trigger each, supply the decision functions, and WIRE them into tools.extdeps_scope_placement_gate so the refusal fires per PR instead of describing itself. IT DOES NOT: move any file, edit any importer, or touch src/v2. The operator was explicit that the full migration is not required inside the D0 citation change, and the src/v2 pass — src/v2/extdeps/languages is entirely flat, with bash alone spread across six modules — is a large namespace operation that belongs in its own change. WHY THE MOVES ARE NOT MADE HERE, and this is a mechanical reason rather than a scheduling preference: all four flat modules are rows of the FROZEN legacy manifest in gunbc.extdeps_scope_frontier. Moving one deletes its manifest row and creates an ADDED dag/extdeps path, and the frontier law states that an added file's only landing states are scope carrier or genuine machinery — it cannot rejoin the frozen manifest. So each move would force its surface module to adopt an extdeps_model_scope and spell a second literal ExternalAuthority for an upstream whose subject carrier D0 just authored. Whether one upstream may be cited once and shared, or must be re-declared per module, is the exact question currently escalated to the operator in extdeps.posix.shell_command_language posix_authority_duplication_note, unresolved. Performing the moves inside this change would answer it unilaterally, in four places, as a side effect of tidying directories. The homing obligation is therefore recorded and walled; the migration waits on that ruling."

