type FlatModuleVerdict
  = FlatModuleOnFrontier { file_path: String, moves_to: String }
  | FlatModuleRefused { file_path: String }

type HomedModuleVerdict
  = ModuleAtCanonicalHome { file_path: String, canonical_prefix: String }
  | ModuleOutsideItsHome { file_path: String, expected_prefix: String }

data placement_check_is_not_identity_note: String = "THE PREFIX IS CHECKED AGAINST A DECLARED PAIRING, NEVER USED TO DERIVE ONE, and the distinction is the whole of the operator's fusion warning. module_at_canonical_home takes a target's home row and asks whether a file the caller has ALREADY associated with that target sits at its address. The target arrives as a DeclarationRef; the prefix answers only 'is this placed correctly', never 'which target is this'. There is deliberately no function here that maps a path to a target, because that function is what would make position compete with the symbolic key — a module misfiled under another target's directory must be REFUSED, not silently reclassified as belonging to the directory it sits in. That is also why the refusal carries the EXPECTED prefix rather than a discovered one: the expectation comes from the declared home, so the diagnostic points at the declaration a reader can check. WHY THIS EXISTS AT ALL, honestly: an earlier revision of this module walled only flat modules, on the mistaken premise that std carries no prefix predicate. It does — starts_with is a registered PrimitiveContract in std.primitives with a template row in std.algebra and live production use — and the gap that premise left was real: a flat module was refused while a target-specific module filed under the WRONG target's directory was never consulted. The operator's law is that a target-specific module outside its canonical prefix refuses, which is this arm, not only the flat one."

data flat_module_refusal_note: String = "THE WALL, AND WHY ITS FAILURE ARM REFUSES RATHER THAN WIDENS. A flat language module is admitted ONLY by exact membership in the frontier above; anything else is FlatModuleRefused carrying the offending path, which is typed and located rather than a Bool. There is no arm that admits an unrecognised flat module, no allowance keyed on a name pattern, and no toggle — the three shapes DESIGN section 5 rules out. THE CONSEQUENCE, ENFORCED RATHER THAN INTENDED: a new target-specific module authored flat beside the target directories cannot land, so the population this frontier freezes can only shrink, because tools.extdeps_scope_placement_gate calls added_language_paths_refused on the paths this change adds and exits failure naming them. AN EARLIER REVISION CLAIMED THAT CONSEQUENCE WHILE NOTHING CALLED THE PREDICATE (cursor review 48516, and the parent lane found it independently). That is the coverage-by-illusion tier DESIGN section 6 names — machinery exists, nothing gates on it, and an inert lens is itself a lie — with the sentence asserting the wall being the only thing that made it look otherwise. Caught in review, not by me. A module inside a target directory is not a flat module and is never consulted here, which is the whole point — the wall constrains where NEW surfaces go without touching the twenty-five directories D0 created."

fn classify_flat_module(file_path: String) -> FlatModuleVerdict {
  fold(
    legacy_language_module_homes,
    init: FlatModuleRefused { file_path: file_path },
    f: fn(acc, r) {
      if r.file_path == file_path {
        FlatModuleOnFrontier { file_path: r.file_path, moves_to: r.moves_to }
      } else {
        acc
      }
    }
  )
}

fn flat_module_admitted(file_path: String) -> Bool {
  match classify_flat_module(file_path: file_path) {
    FlatModuleOnFrontier { file_path: _, moves_to: _ } => true,
    FlatModuleRefused { file_path: _ } => false
  }
}

fn every_flat_module_is_on_the_frontier(walked_flat_paths: List<String>) -> Bool {
  all(walked_flat_paths, p => flat_module_admitted(file_path: p))
}

data language_root_prefix: String = "dag/extdeps/languages/"

data added_path_admission_note: String = "THE GATE PREDICATE — what the per-PR wall actually calls, and why it is shaped as an admission rather than a flatness test. A path added under languages/ is ADMITTED if it sits inside some declared target home, inside a declared directory whose target is unregistered, or is one of the four exact legacy frontier rows. Everything else under that root REFUSES. The shape matters: there is no function here that decides whether a path is 'flat', because deciding that from a string means segment-splitting the path and then reasoning about what the segment denotes, which is one short step from reading a target off a directory name — the fusion this module exists to avoid. Admission against declared rosters gets the same outcome with the identity question never asked. THE CONSEQUENCE, and it is now real rather than intended: a new module authored directly in dag/extdeps/languages/ is in none of the three rosters, so tools.extdeps_scope_placement_gate refuses the change. The population the frontier freezes can only shrink. A NEW TARGET DIRECTORY ALSO REFUSES UNTIL ITS HOME IS DECLARED, and that is deliberate rather than incidental: adding a target's surfaces without saying where that target lives is the state this wall exists to prevent, and the fix is one declared row in the same change."

fn path_under_any_declared_home(file_path: String) -> Bool {
  fold(language_target_homes, init: false, f: fn(acc, h) {
    acc || starts_with(s: file_path, prefix: h.canonical_prefix)
  })
}

fn path_under_unregistered_target_directory(file_path: String) -> Bool {
  fold(unregistered_target_directories, init: false, f: fn(acc, d) {
    acc || starts_with(s: file_path, prefix: d.prefix)
  })
}

fn added_language_path_admitted(file_path: String) -> Bool {
  if starts_with(s: file_path, prefix: language_root_prefix) {
    path_under_any_declared_home(file_path: file_path)
      || path_under_unregistered_target_directory(file_path: file_path)
      || flat_module_admitted(file_path: file_path)
  } else {
    true
  }
}

fn added_language_paths_refused(added: List<String>) -> List<String> {
  fold(added, init: [], f: fn(acc, p) {
    if added_language_path_admitted(file_path: p) { acc } else { concat(acc, [p]) }
  })
}

fn module_at_canonical_home(file_path: String, home: LanguageTargetHome) -> HomedModuleVerdict {
  if starts_with(s: file_path, prefix: home.canonical_prefix) {
    ModuleAtCanonicalHome { file_path: file_path, canonical_prefix: home.canonical_prefix }
  } else {
    ModuleOutsideItsHome { file_path: file_path, expected_prefix: home.canonical_prefix }
  }
}

fn module_home_admitted(file_path: String, home: LanguageTargetHome) -> Bool {
  match module_at_canonical_home(file_path: file_path, home: home) {
    ModuleAtCanonicalHome { file_path: _, canonical_prefix: _ } => true,
    ModuleOutsideItsHome { file_path: _, expected_prefix: _ } => false
  }
}

fn legacy_flat_module_count() -> Int {
  fold(legacy_language_module_homes, init: 0, f: fn(acc, r) { acc + 1 })
}

data language_module_home_maximum_conclusion: String = "MAXIMUM CONCLUSION — what a green check here establishes and what it cannot. IT ESTABLISHES that every flat language module named by the caller is an exact frontier row, so a new flat module refuses. IT DOES NOT ESTABLISH that the four rows are the complete flat population of the live tree. The gate reads the paths a CHANGE ADDS, at merge-base grain, so it governs what enters and says nothing about what already sits there: a flat module predating this wall and missing from the frontier is invisible to it. That is the same grain split the scope frontier states about itself, and it is why this module gates the DIRECTION of change rather than certifying the current tree — a claim true only because the gate arm now exists, and false in the revision that first made it. NOR DOES IT ESTABLISH THAT A FILE SITS UNDER THE RIGHT TARGET'S DIRECTORY, which is the sharpest limit and belongs here rather than only in the mechanism note (cursor review 48555). The gate admits a path found under SOME declared home; it cannot ask whether that home is the one belonging to the file's target, because the gate sees a path and no declared pairing for it. So an emitter for one language placed under another language's directory PASSES the gate. module_at_canonical_home does decide that case correctly and is exercised both ways in the witness, but it needs the caller to supply which home to check against, and the added-path observation supplies no such thing. THIS IS A BOUNDARY, NOT A FAIL-OPEN, and the distinction is that closing it requires deriving a target from a path — the one move this module refuses on purpose, because it is what makes position compete with the symbolic key. The honest closure is a declared pairing at authoring time, not a cleverer string test; until a change can state which target a new module belongs to, the gate's reach ends at 'inside some target's directory rather than loose in the root'. NOR DOES IT ESTABLISH that any target's surfaces are complete or correctly grouped — only that a target-specific module is not authored flat. The construction-side end state is the same one the module-identity-versus-storage lane names: when a module's home is derived from the containment tree rather than declared beside it, this frontier and its wall both delete."
